# Explore Quantum Surreal Observation

Begin with the central distinction: **labels name outcomes; amplitudes carry probability**. The corrected epsilon state places the infinitesimal in the amplitude. Use the first laboratory to watch the weak Born weight fall quadratically while normalization remains fixed.

{{interactive_demo:0}}

{{visualization:0}}

<details><summary>Reveal the normalization and collapse argument</summary>
For amplitudes $1$ and $arepsilon$, the squared norm is $1+arepsilon^2$. The exact weights are $1/(1+arepsilon^2)$ and $arepsilon^2/(1+arepsilon^2)$. Standard part retains the first as $1$ and sends the second to $0$. By contrast, equal nonzero amplitudes give $a^2/(2a^2)=1/2$ on both labels.
</details>

## Exact finite infinitesimal probability

The reservoir construction keeps infinitesimal mass algebraically exact. Build events below and compare their pair-valued mass with the ordinary mass visible after standard part.

{{interactive_demo:1}}

{{algorithm:1}}

{{visualization:1}}

<details><summary>Reveal the Dirac-collapse theorem</summary>
With $n$ visible atoms, an event containing $k$ visible atoms and reservoir flag $b$ has mass $(b,k-nb)$. Standard part selects the first coordinate, so the observed event probability is exactly $b$: one precisely when the reservoir is present and zero otherwise.
</details>

## The tropical shadow

Max-plus mathematics retains the largest penalized score. It agrees with reservoir support when every visible score obeys $f(v_i)+M\le f(r)$, but it can escape when an observable overcomes the penalty.

{{interactive_demo:2}}

{{algorithm:2}}

{{visualization:2}}

<details><summary>Reveal why the bridge is conditional</summary>
Standard part is additive and removes infinitesimal coordinates. Tropical integration is max-plus linear and compares $f(r)$ with $f(v_i)+M$. If any visible score is larger, it wins tropically despite carrying zero standard-part mass. The two procedures share a support law only inside the dominance cone.
</details>

